1 LINK

   Links object modules (.OBJ files, such as MACRO writes) into a VAX/VMS
   executable image (.EXE) that RUN can run, or into a shareable image
   (/SHAREABLE) whose routines and data other images use.  Its images
   and maps are laid out as VMS's LINK lays them out, so an image made
   here runs on VMS, and VMS's objects link here.

   Format

     LINK filespec[,...]

2 Parameter

 filespec[,...]

   Object files (.OBJ), each of which may be followed by a qualifier that
   makes it another kind of input: /LIBRARY, /INCLUDE, /SELECTIVE_SEARCH,
   or /OPTIONS (see Qualifiers).  Files follow MACRO's rules for host
   files and files on mounted volumes (see MACRO Parameter), and a later
   file's bare name is found beside the one before it.

2 Description

   Program sections are laid out as VMS LINK lays them out: grouped into
   image sections by their attributes, from address 200 (hexadecimal) in
   an executable and 0 in a shareable image, cluster by cluster: the
   options' clusters, then the default cluster, then a cluster for each
   shareable image.  Writable pages nothing is stored in become
   demand-zero.

   A global symbol the objects don't define is looked up, in order, in
   the command's libraries, the user libraries (/USERLIBRARY), and then:

     IMAGELIB.OLB    Which shareable image defines the routine.  Its
                     offset there comes from the image itself
                     (SYS$SHARE:LIBRTL.EXE, say) or, with no such file,
                     from govax's own tables.
     STARLET.OLB     The module that defines the symbol is added to the
                     image, as VMS LINK does.
     govax's tables  Every LIBRTL routine, and the other run-time library
                     routines govax provides, in their shareable images;
                     FTNRTL's routines (FTN$...); STARLET's status codes
                     and other definitions (SS$_..., RMS$_..., IO$_...,
                     and more); and the system services (SYS$...), at
                     their addresses in the P1 vector.

   The two libraries and VMS's shareable images are VMS files, which
   govax doesn't include.  LINK looks for them as
   SYS$LIBRARY:IMAGELIB.OLB, SYS$LIBRARY:STARLET.OLB, and
   SYS$SHARE:image.EXE on a mounted volume, then in the host directory
   the vax.library setting names (or the older vax.link.library).
   Without them, govax's own tables, which were captured from those
   files, give the same image for everything they define.

   A routine in a shareable image is reached with a general mode operand
   (CALLS #1,G^LIB$PUT_OUTPUT) or a .ADDRESS of it; the image reaches it
   through its fixup section, and RUN connects it to the image, or for
   LIBRTL and the system's other images, to govax's own routines (see
   RUN Shareable_images).

   A symbol nothing defines is a warning, as in VMS LINK: it is 0, each
   reference to it is reported, and the image is still written.  A
   symbol referred to only weakly (.WEAK) is 0 without a warning.  An
   image with no transfer address is written with a warning too.

   With /TRACEBACK, the objects' traceback records become the image's
   debug symbol table, after the image's other blocks, as VMS LINK
   writes it.  The debugger reads it (SHOW CALLS names each routine and
   line), and an unhandled condition's traceback dump names the
   routines and lines, on govax as on VMS.  Debugger records (from
   MACRO/DEBUG) are skipped unless the link is /DEBUG.

   From the host shell, "govax link filespec..." runs this command, with
   --executable file, --shareable, --no-executable, --symbol-table file,
   --no-traceback, --debug, --no-syslib, --no-userlibrary, --map,
   --map-file file, --brief, --full, --library file, or --options file.

2 Options_file

   An options file (.OPT, named in the command with /OPTIONS) has a line
   for each option or list of input files.  An exclamation point starts
   a comment, and a hyphen at a line's end continues it.  Its files may
   have /LIBRARY, /INCLUDE, and /SELECTIVE_SEARCH, and also /SHAREABLE, a
   shareable image (.EXE) whose symbols the image uses, found as RUN
   would find it, so a logical name of its name or SYS$SHARE leads to
   it.  A host path is written in quotes, for its slashes.
   SYS$INPUT/OPTIONS reads the options from SYS$INPUT: in a command
   procedure, the data lines after the LINK command.  Options and
   keywords may be abbreviated.  The options are VAX LINK's:

     STACK=pages             The user stack's first size (default 20).
                             With paging on it grows past that by
                             itself, to about 10 MB with the defaults;
                             with paging off the stack is a fixed 2 MB
                             of P1, and STACK= is ignored.
     IDENTIFICATION=id       The image's identification.
     NAME=name               The image's name in its header.
     SYMBOL=name,value       Defines a global symbol, whatever the
                             objects say.
     UNIVERSAL=name[,...]    Makes symbols universal: a shareable image's
                             symbols other images may use.
     GSMATCH=match,major,minor
                             A shareable image's match control (ALWAYS,
                             EQUAL, or LEQUAL) and ident; RUN checks it.
                             Without it the ident comes from the link
                             time, and the match is EQUAL, so a program
                             must be relinked when the image is.
     CLUSTER=name[,base][,pfc][,file...]
                             A cluster of image sections: the files
                             named, placed together and in this order, at
                             an address if base is given.
     COLLECT=cluster,psect[,...]
                             Moves program sections into a cluster.
     PSECT_ATTR=psect,attribute[,...]
                             Changes a program section's attributes (PIC,
                             SHR, WRT, EXE, RD, GBL, ... and their NO
                             forms, or an alignment).
     BASE=address            The image's base address.  An executable
                             linked at a base places its shareable images
                             at link time too.
     DZRO_MIN=pages          The fewest pages of a writable section's
                             uninitialized run that become a demand-zero
                             section of their own.
     ISD_MAX=count           The most image sections an executable may
                             have before demand-zero runs stay with their
                             sections (ignored, with OPTIGNSHR, for a
                             shareable image).
     PROTECT=YES|NO          Marks the image sections of the clusters
                             that follow as protected, as VMS needs for a
                             shareable image installed /PROTECT (govax
                             has no INSTALL; RUN ignores it).
     IOSEGMENT=pages[,[NO]P0BUFS]
                             The image I/O segment's size.

   UNSUPPORTED= is refused.  Numbers are decimal, or hexadecimal or octal
   after %X or %O.  A bad option or value is %LINK-F-OPTSYNERR or
   OPTVALERR, quoting it.

2 Shareable_images

   A library of routines linked /SHAREABLE has, in the usual pattern, a
   transfer vector first, so that its routines keep their addresses from
   one version of the image to the next:

     .TRANSFER   MY_ROUTINE          ; MY_ROUTINE is universal
     .MASK       MY_ROUTINE          ; the routine's entry mask
     JMP         L^MY_ROUTINE+2      ; and a jump past it

   with an options file such as

     CLUSTER=TRANSFER_VECTOR,,,VECTOR
     GSMATCH=LEQUAL,1,1000
     UNIVERSAL=MY_DATA

   A program uses it by naming it in an options file (MYSHR/SHAREABLE),
   and reaches its routines and data with general mode operands
   (CALLS #0,G^MY_ROUTINE) or a .ADDRESS of them.  The image's GSMATCH=
   decides which later versions the program accepts: with LEQUAL, any
   with the same major id and a higher or equal minor id.  A shareable
   image may itself use others.  LIBRARY/CREATE/SHARE makes a library of
   shareable images, which LINK searches for the images that define
   symbols.

2 Qualifiers
/BRIEF

   Makes the map brief: only the object modules and the image synopsis.

/[NO]DEBUG

   Puts the objects' debugger records (from MACRO/DEBUG) in the debug
   symbol table too, and marks the image for the debugger, as VMS LINK
   does.  /DEBUG includes /TRACEBACK, even if /NOTRACEBACK is given.
   RUN starts such an image under the debugger.  A user-written debugger
   module (/DEBUG=file) isn't supported.  /NODEBUG is the default.

/[NO]EXECUTABLE[=filespec]

   Writes the image (the default).  Without a file name, the image has
   the first object's name and location, with the type EXE in the same
   case as the object's type.  A name that is only a directory gets that
   name in it.  /NOEXECUTABLE links and reports any errors, but writes
   no image.

/FULL

   Makes the map full: it also lists the image sections (each cluster's
   sections, their types, and a shareable image's global section names,
   matches, and idents), and the program sections and symbols the
   libraries and shareable images gave.

/HOST

   After a file, makes every file a host path, whatever it looks like.

/INCLUDE=(module[,...])

   After a library, adds those modules of it to the image: of a
   shareable image library, those images, as an options file's
   /SHAREABLE does.  With /LIBRARY too, the library is also searched.

/LIBRARY

   After a file, makes it an object library (.OLB) searched for symbols
   the objects don't define, before the system libraries, or a shareable
   image library (LIBRARY/SHARE, or IMAGELIB).  A shareable image
   library's images are looked for beside it, then in SYS$LIBRARY, then
   as RUN finds them.

/[NO]MAP[=filespec]

   Writes a link map, laid out as VMS LINK lays out its default map: the
   object modules, the program sections and each module's part of them,
   the global symbols the objects define, and the image synopsis.
   Without a file name, the map has the first object's name and
   location, with the type MAP.  /NOMAP, the default, writes none.

/OPTIONS

   After a file, makes it an options file (.OPT) (see Options_file).

/SELECTIVE_SEARCH

   After an object, takes only its definitions of symbols something else
   refers to.

/SHAREABLE[=filespec]

   Makes a shareable image: one with no transfer address, linked at
   address 0 so that RUN can put it anywhere, whose universal symbols
   (the options' UNIVERSAL= and the objects' .TRANSFER) are in its
   global symbol table for other links to use.  The image is named as
   /EXECUTABLE names it (one or the other may name it, not both).  See
   Shareable_images.

/SYMBOL_TABLE[=filespec]

   Also writes the image's global symbol table as an object module file
   (by default the first object's name with the type STB), which another
   link can take as an object.

/[NO]SYSLIB

   Searches IMAGELIB.OLB and STARLET.OLB for symbols the objects don't
   define, as VMS LINK does by default.  /NOSYSLIB uses only govax's own
   tables.

/[NO]TRACEBACK

   /TRACEBACK, the default, starts the image through SYS$IMGSTA, as VMS
   LINK does, and gives it a debug symbol table built from the objects'
   traceback records.  /NOTRACEBACK starts it at its own transfer
   address, with no debug symbol table.

/[NO]USERLIBRARY[=(table[,...])]

   Searches the default user libraries, after the command's own
   libraries and before the system's: the object or shareable image
   libraries the logical names LNK$LIBRARY, LNK$LIBRARY_1, ...
   LNK$LIBRARY_999 name, taken in turn until one isn't defined.  The
   tables are PROCESS, GROUP, and SYSTEM, searched in that order; ALL,
   the default, is all three.  /NOUSERLIBRARY (or NONE) searches none.

2 Examples

   1.$ MACRO HELLO
     $ LINK HELLO
     $ RUN HELLO

     Assembles, links, and runs HELLO.

   2.$ LINK DUA1:[OBJ]MAIN,SUB /EXECUTABLE=DUA1:[EXE]PROG.EXE

     Links two objects into PROG.EXE in another directory.

   3.$ LINK MAIN,MYLIB/LIBRARY,PROG/OPTIONS /MAP

     Links MAIN, searching MYLIB.OLB, with PROG.OPT's options and a map.

   4.$ LINK/SHAREABLE=MYSHR/MAP/FULL VECTOR,ROUTINES,MYSHR/OPTIONS
     $ DEFINE MYSHR DUA1:[WORK]MYSHR.EXE
     $ LINK MAIN,SYS$INPUT/OPTIONS
     MYSHR/SHAREABLE
     $ RUN MAIN

     In a procedure: makes a shareable image, then links and runs a
     program that uses it.
